# starter.feeds — the shipped feeds-and-speeds table (K3, DECISIONS.md →
# "K3, the material…", fork 3B).
#
# These are CONSERVATIVE STARTING POINTS, chosen to rub rather than break,
# cross-checked against published handbook ranges — they are not optimized
# numbers and were never meant to be. Carbide tooling is assumed and said so
# in every source line (tool material is a K2-side axis, absent in v1). A
# material we have not vetted is ABSENT from this file, not guessed: the miss
# refuses by name and lists what the table does hold.
#
# Your proven numbers outrank ours: declare `feeds_table "<path>"` in the
# program and a shop record shadows the shipped record WHOLE at
# (material × tool type) granularity — never field-merged.
#
# Grammar (the `.catalog` family):
#   units mm|inch          optional header, before any record; default mm.
#                          inch means surface_speed in SFM, chipload in
#                          in/tooth, band diameters in inches — converted
#                          once at parse, downstream is always metric.
#   feeds <material> <tool-type>   opens a record
#   surface_speed <m/min>          required, positive
#   source <free text>             required — every number says where it
#                                  came from, or the record refuses
#   chipload <dia-lo>..<dia-hi> <mm/tooth>   repeated; a band covers
#                                  dia-lo <= dia < dia-hi; overlap refuses;
#                                  between-band diameters are a miss, never
#                                  an interpolation.

units mm

feeds 6061 Endmill
surface_speed 150
source conservative start, low end of published carbide ranges for 6061-T6 (handbook 150-300 m/min); carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.010
chipload 3..6 0.020
chipload 6..13 0.040

feeds 6061 Drill
surface_speed 90
source conservative start, low end of published carbide drilling ranges for 6061-T6; carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.020
chipload 3..6 0.050
chipload 6..13 0.080

feeds 1018 Endmill
surface_speed 80
source conservative start, low end of published carbide ranges for 1018 mild steel (handbook 90-150 m/min); carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.008
chipload 3..6 0.015
chipload 6..13 0.030

feeds 1018 Drill
surface_speed 50
source conservative start, low end of published carbide drilling ranges for 1018 mild steel; carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.015
chipload 3..6 0.040
chipload 6..13 0.060

feeds 304 Endmill
surface_speed 45
source conservative start, low end of published carbide ranges for 304 stainless (handbook 50-100 m/min, work-hardens if rubbed — do not baby the chip load); carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.008
chipload 3..6 0.015
chipload 6..13 0.030

feeds 304 Drill
surface_speed 25
source conservative start, low end of published carbide drilling ranges for 304 stainless; carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.015
chipload 3..6 0.040
chipload 6..13 0.060

feeds 360-brass Endmill
surface_speed 150
source conservative start, low end of published carbide ranges for C360 free-machining brass; carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.010
chipload 3..6 0.020
chipload 6..13 0.040

feeds 360-brass Drill
surface_speed 90
source conservative start, low end of published carbide drilling ranges for C360 free-machining brass; carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.020
chipload 3..6 0.050
chipload 6..13 0.080

feeds acetal Endmill
surface_speed 200
source conservative start for acetal (Delrin), well under published limits (300+ m/min); heat is the failure mode, sharp carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.020
chipload 3..6 0.050
chipload 6..13 0.100

feeds acetal Drill
surface_speed 150
source conservative start for acetal (Delrin) drilling; heat is the failure mode, sharp carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.030
chipload 3..6 0.080
chipload 6..13 0.120

feeds HDPE Endmill
surface_speed 200
source conservative start for HDPE, well under published limits; melting is the failure mode, sharp carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.020
chipload 3..6 0.050
chipload 6..13 0.100

feeds HDPE Drill
surface_speed 150
source conservative start for HDPE drilling; melting is the failure mode, sharp carbide assumed
chipload 1..3 0.030
chipload 3..6 0.080
chipload 6..13 0.120
